      "text_raw": "The same circuit is shown\ntwice, once during clock\nphase 1 (on the left) and\nonce during clock phase 2\n(on the right).\nIn both clock phases, the\ncharges are written on both\ncapacitors aC and C. The\nequation of the sum of\nthe charges, shows that the\noffset voltage v cancels\nos\naltogether. Indeed, it\nappears in all terms. As a\nresult it cancels out.\nThis technique is also\nused to cancel the 1/f noise\nof MOST amplifiers. At very low frequencies, 1/f noise resembles offset. It is now cancelled out\nat the cost of a small increase of the thermal noise.",
      "text": "The same circuit is shown twice, once during clock phase 1 (on the left) and once during clock phase 2 (on the right). In both clock phases, the charges are written on both capacitors aC and C. The equation of the sum of the charges, shows that the offset voltage v cancels os altogether. Indeed, it appears in all terms. As a result it cancels out. This technique is also used to cancel the 1/f noise of MOST amplifiers. At very low frequencies, 1/f noise resembles offset. It is now cancelled out at the cost of a small increase of the thermal noise."
    }
